    "body_en": "<p>TOI-199b is a warm Saturn-sized exoplanet 330 light-years away. New observations by the Webb space telescope show there is methane in its atmosphere. The post Meet a Saturn-sized exoplanet with Earth-like temperatures first appeared on. The institutional report frames the development in practical terms and ties it to the broader mission or observing effort.</p><p>That matters because exoplanet science has moved beyond the era of simple discovery into a period of comparative characterization. With more than five thousand confirmed planets known, the scientifically productive questions now concern atmospheric composition, internal structure, orbital history and the statistical properties of populations rather than the existence of individual worlds. A new detection or spectral measurement is most valuable when it adds a well-constrained data point to those comparative frameworks, not when it stands alone as an anecdote. The post Meet a Saturn-sized exoplanet with Earth-like temperatures first appeared on EarthSky. A new study from PennState University shows that one such world, the Saturn-sized exoplanet TOI-199b, has near-earthly temperatures and a methane atmosphere.</p><p>TOI-199b is a Saturn-sized exoplanet 330 light-years from Earth. The planet, TOI-199b, is about 330 light-years from Earth.</p><p>Since the first exoplanet was discovered in 1992 by a team that included Aleksander Wolszczan at Penn State, astronomers have found thousands of exoplanets. Atmosphere Of Saturn-sized Planet With Earth-like Temperature Contains Methaneastrobiology. com/2026/05/atmo. #astrobiology #AbSciCon #AbSciCon26 #exoplanet, Astrobiology.</p><p>The highest recorded temperature on Earth is about 57 degrees Celsius (134 degrees F). When the planet transits, passes in front of, its star as seen from Earth, Webb analyzes light from the star as it passes through the planet’s atmosphere.</p><p>The broader interest lies in making the target less anecdotal and more comparable with the rest of the known planetary population. Population-level questions, such as the frequency of atmospheres around small rocky planets or the prevalence of water-rich worlds in the habitable zone, require well-characterized individual data points before statistical patterns become meaningful. Each new planet with a measured radius, mass and, ideally, atmospheric constraint is a brick in that larger structure, and the accumulation of bricks eventually allows theorists to test formation models against real distributions rather than projections.</p><p>Lead author Aaron Bello-Arufe, a postdoctoral researcher at NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory, said: As a planet passes in front of its star, some of the star’s light passes. Specific elements will absorb specific wavelengths of light, creating a fingerprint in the spectrum of light that JWST detects that reflects the atmosphere’s composition.</p><p>Because the account originates with EarthSky, it functions best as a primary institutional report that is close to the data and operations, not as independent scientific validation. Institutional communications are produced by organizations with legitimate interests in presenting their work in a favorable light, which does not make them unreliable but does make them partial. Details that complicate the narrative, including instrument limitations, unexpected failures and results below projections, tend to be minimized relative to progress messages. Technical documentation and peer-reviewed publications, where they exist, provide the complementary layer that institutional releases cannot substitute.</p><p>The next step is to improve independent constraints on the mass, radius, atmospheric composition and orbital dynamics of the target. Transmission spectroscopy with JWST, radial velocity campaigns with high-resolution ground-based spectrographs and phase-curve measurements from space photometry represent the observational toolkit that can move characterization from plausible to robust. That convergence of techniques is the standard the community now expects before a planetary atmosphere result is treated as confirmed.</p><p class=\"art-source\"><a href=\"https://earthsky.org/space/saturn-sized-exoplanet-earth-like-temperatures-toi-199b-methane/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Source</a></p>",
